# artifact-style — the rules a published page is checked against

Claude Code's own `artifact-design` and `artifact-diagramming` skills decide how much design a
page deserves and what a diagram should show. They say "clipped text is a bug" and "wide content
scrolls in its own container", and stop there. This skill is the layer under that: the concrete
fix for each kind of text that outgrows its box, one wrapper that makes every diagram zoomable,
a font and palette decision that starts from the project rather than from taste, and a lint that
fails the page before anyone squints at it. Load the native skill first; this one assumes it.

**Scope: the Artifact surface only.** Mermaid inside a vault note stays unstyled
(`strategy-coach:blueprint` says why — Obsidian, a pull request and an Artifact each theme it
differently). Everything here applies to the single HTML file the Artifact tool publishes.

**Precedence, same as artifact-design's: the user's words, then the project's own system, then
the fallback.** IBM Plex on the sea-to-lime palette is what a page gets when the repo declares
nothing — never a house style to impose on a project that has one.

## Steps

1. **Detect the project's system** — `$AICOACH_REPO/plugins/design-coach/skills/artifact-style/references/detect.md`. At most six file reads: Tailwind
   config or `@theme`, `:root` custom properties, a theme object, a tokens export, the font
   `<link>`s, a logo. Stop at the first source that answers; note `file:line`.
2. **Write the design brief** before any HTML — five lines: sans and mono stacks, the six to
   eight semantic tokens for light and dark, and where each came from (`file:line` or
   `fallback`). This is the input to artifact-design's own pre-code design plan; show it in the
   reply so the reader can see what the page inherited.
3. **Build from `$AICOACH_REPO/plugins/design-coach/skills/artifact-style/references/skeleton.html`.** It is the one copy of the base CSS (tokens in all
   three theme states, overflow classes, table wrapper, code blocks, the zoomable figure) and the
   zoom script. Copy it, set the title, then either replace the `§tokens` section with
   `$AICOACH_REPO/plugins/design-coach/skills/artifact-style/references/palettes/sage.css` (the skeleton carries `sea`) or overwrite the hex with the
   brief's project values — names stay — and delete what the page does not use. Never paste a
   second copy of the CSS from memory; the lint is written against the skeleton's names.
4. **Lint, then fix.** `node "$AICOACH_REPO/plugins/design-coach/scripts/check-artifact.js" <page.html>`
   — every `error:` is fixed before publishing; every `warn:` is either fixed or named in the
   reply. Show the summary line. The lint is Node-only; a harness without Node applies the
   rules below by hand and says so.
5. **Publish** with the native tool as usual — favicon, stable file path on re-runs, the design
   brief in the reply. A `PreToolUse` hook shipped with this plugin runs the same lint on the
   file the Artifact tool is about to publish and asks before a page with errors goes out.

## The four rule sets

### 1. Text never leaves its box — `$AICOACH_REPO/plugins/design-coach/skills/artifact-style/references/overflow.md`

The root cause is almost always one of two things: a flex or grid child that defaults to
`min-width: auto` and refuses to shrink, or a long unbreakable token (URL, hash, identifier).
The skeleton sets `min-width: 0` on `.row > *` and `.grid > *` and `overflow-wrap: anywhere` on
`body`. From there, choose the fix by what the text is, not by where it overflowed:

| Content | Fix | Never |
|---|---|---|
| table cell | a `.trunc` block inside the `td` + `title="full text"`; the table in `.tablewrap` | wrap a wide table onto the page; `.trunc` on the `td` itself (it ignores `max-width`) |
| card / section title | `.clamp2` + `title` | let it push the card taller than its row |
| URL, hash, id | `.anywhere` — break it | hide it: a truncated URL is unrecoverable |
| code | `pre` scrolls, `white-space: pre` | wrap code |
| diagram label | shorten the words or shrink the font | truncate — an ellipsised node is meaningless |
| KPI number | `.kpi` shrinks with `clamp()` — in a card | clamp lines; put it in a table (every cell shares one font size) |

WCAG 1.4.10 is the license: tables, diagrams and code may scroll in two dimensions; everything
else reflows at 320 px.

### 2. Every diagram is zoomable — `$AICOACH_REPO/plugins/design-coach/skills/artifact-style/references/diagrams.md`

Inline SVG or a mermaid block, it goes inside `figure.zoomable` from the skeleton: caption
stating the claim, a viewport of **fixed height** (`--zoom-height` per figure — zooming changes
what is in the box, never the box, so the page does not jump), the drawing, and the control
cluster markdown viewers already use — icon buttons overlaid bottom-right: zoom out, level,
zoom in, reset, fullscreen. The script sizes the svg in CSS pixels — no transforms, so the
scroll box grows with the zoom and text stays sharp. Ctrl/⌘+wheel and pinch zoom around the
cursor; drag pans; a plain wheel scrolls the viewport; `+ − 0 1` work from the keyboard. Mermaid is rendered by the host into an
`svg` under your `<pre class="mermaid">` — the script waits for it with a `MutationObserver`,
and grows mermaid's type with `%%{init:{'themeVariables':{'fontSize':'26px'}}}%%` because the
host's default is unreadable at fit-to-width. Nothing is added inside the `<svg>` itself;
artifact-diagramming's ban on script and style there stands.

Drawing rules the native skill leaves implicit: ≤ 7 nodes per view (split past that), a verb
or protocol on every arrow, labels ≥ 12 px at 1:1, a legend only when the same encoding repeats
across figures, and a caption that states the takeaway rather than the title.

### 3. Fonts: the project's, else IBM Plex, else Arial — `$AICOACH_REPO/plugins/design-coach/skills/artifact-style/references/tokens.md`

A detected font is used with its own fallbacks appended. Otherwise the skeleton's stacks:
`"IBM Plex Sans", Inter, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, Arial, sans-serif` and
`"IBM Plex Mono", "JetBrains Mono", ui-monospace, Consolas, Menlo, monospace`, loaded from
Google Fonts — the one font host the Artifact CSP admits — with `display=swap`. Body 16 px /
1.55, measure 60–72 ch, headings on a 1.25 scale. Chart and diagram text follows the page's
mono stack: the native dataviz skill's `system-ui` for chart type is overridden here so one
page has one voice.

### 4. Palette: the project's, else one of two, dark mode designed not derived — `$AICOACH_REPO/plugins/design-coach/skills/artifact-style/references/tokens.md`

Semantic names, never raw hex in components: `--bg --surface --surface-2 --border
--border-strong --text --muted --accent --accent-hover --accent-contrast --accent-2
--accent-2-ink`. The native three-state pattern (light on bare `:root`, dark under the media
query guarded by `:root:not([data-theme="light"])`, dark again under `:root[data-theme="dark"]`)
is followed verbatim — the lint checks that the same names appear in all three. Two fallbacks
ship as drop-in token files under `$AICOACH_REPO/plugins/design-coach/skills/artifact-style/references/palettes/`, each placed by measured contrast so
every text/ground pair clears 4.5:1 in both themes:

- **sea** (`palettes/sea.css`, what the skeleton carries) — sea `#05668d`, teal `#028090`,
  green `#00a896`, mint `#02c39a`, lime `#f0f3bd`; dark on a navy ground `#00132d…#00377e`
  with cool off-white ink and mint links. Cool and crisp: for systems — architecture, APIs,
  pipelines, security.
- **sage** (`palettes/sage.css`) — pale sage `#cad2c5`, sage `#84a98c`, green `#52796f`, slate
  `#354f52`, charcoal `#2f3e46`; dark on the charcoal one step down. Muted and natural: for
  people and process — blueprints, onboarding, research briefs, long prose.

Pick once per project, name it in the brief (`fallback: sea` / `fallback: sage`), keep it across
that project's pages; the default when nothing points either way is sea. Dark mode is a second
palette, not an inversion: never `#000`, a lighter surface for elevation, the lighter accent.
Three hues plus neutrals at most on any one page; categorical series from Okabe-Ito; hue never
carries magnitude.

